      // Two independently-read sources on the same specific December 1938 episode. Otto Frisch's own firsthand account (quoted at length in Libby's The Uranium People, reproduced by the National Museum of Nuclear Science & History) has Meitner rejecting the easy way out when Frisch proposed Hahn's chemistry might simply be wrong: she "shook her head and said that Hahn was too good a chemist to be wrong; his results must be correct," then insisted on working through why, sitting with Frisch at a tree stump to calculate from the liquid-drop model. APS News's independently-read account confirms she was specifically the one who calculated the ~200 MeV mass-energy defect via E=mc^2 that made the physical explanation quantitatively work ("Meitner determined that the two daughter nuclei together would be less massive... which, when plugged into Einstein's formula... works out to 200 MeV. Everything fit."). Scored from this specific calculation episode, not from the fission discovery's later fame or the Nobel Prize she was denied for it.
      analytical_rigor: [88, 0.68, "d", "A"],
      // Two independently-read sources corroborate the same specific 19-year arc: Chemistry World documents that on arriving in Berlin in 1907 she was, as a woman, "denied access to the university's laboratories" and worked for years as an unofficial, unpaid assistant in Hahn's improvised cellar lab; the Atomic Heritage Foundation's profile independently confirms she did not receive her first paid position until 1912 (age 34), sustaining this uncompensated arrangement for five years before that and continuing to climb the academic ladder for 14 more, until in 1926 she became the first woman in Germany to hold a physics professorship, at the University of Berlin. Scored from this specific, dated institutional trajectory, not from her later scientific reputation.
      persistence: [78, 0.6, "d", "A"],
      // A specific, directly-quoted refusal -- "I will have nothing to do with a bomb!" -- documented identically across two independently-read sources (the Atomic Heritage Foundation's profile and Chemistry World's feature): she declined to join the Allied atomic-bomb effort during World War II, a position that put her at odds with the era's dominant scientific current, including several of her own former collaborators and refugee physicist peers (her nephew Otto Frisch among them) who did join weapons work. Scored as a specific, dated professional/ethical stance, not inferred from her general reputation for integrity.
      independent_thinking: [75, 0.55, "d", "A"],
      // APS News's account, independently corroborated by Chemistry World's account of the same 1938 episode: after Austria's annexation invalidated the citizenship exemption that had protected her, Meitner "had tried to hang on to her position as long as possible" in Berlin rather than leaving immediately when the danger became apparent, and only fled -- suddenly, with just two small suitcases, her passport invalid, assisted across the Dutch border by Dutch colleagues -- once continuing to delay became untenable. Scored dual_edged from this specific, documented sequence of professional decisions (staying, then a sudden forced exit), not from any inference about her private emotional state, per this project's evidence-discipline rule on trauma/persecution history.
      risk_tolerance: [62, 0.45, "d", "D"],
      // Otto Frisch's own firsthand account (via Libby's book, reproduced by the Atomic Heritage Foundation) describes Meitner actively directing their December 1938 collaboration rather than following his lead: he states the fission explanation was "the result of a discussion into which Lise Meitner more or less forced me -- I would much rather have talked about my own work," and that during their subsequent expensive long-distance call to finalize their joint Nature paper, "she had her own suggestions on how we should put matters." A single specific, non-self-sourced incident (Frisch's own testimony, not Meitner's), scored moderately and at reduced confidence because it rests on one episode.
      persuasiveness: [60, 0.42, "d", "N"],
      // Chemistry World's independently-read account documents a specific, sustained 31-year (1907-1938) working partnership with Otto Hahn built on an explicit, complementary division of labor -- "Hahn's skill in isolating and chemically characterising candidates for recognition as elements was complemented by Meitner's flair for detecting and interpreting their radiation signatures" -- that produced joint discoveries (the isotope protactinium, 1917) and continued by correspondence even after her 1938 exile, when Hahn kept sending her his uranium-bombardment data. Kept distinct from the persuasiveness row above: that row scores one specific moment of directive assertiveness; this row scores the sustained structure of a three-decade partnership.
      collaboration: [68, 0.5, "d", "A"],
